{
  "case_id": "2011-10-9995",
  "document_hash": "6c65bc3a65ac746b14744cf76d318e3d50dca1598ddd1d6c68f05a4538ea8a0c",
  "records": [
    {
      "attribution": "Court",
      "case_id": "2011-10-9995",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "6c65bc3a65ac746b14744cf76d318e3d50dca1598ddd1d6c68f05a4538ea8a0c",
      "evidence_id": "ev-6c65bc3a65ac-0000-0000",
      "kind": "procedural_event",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 1,
        "start": 1
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The above-entitled matter came on for oral argument before the Supreme Court of the United States at 11:04 a.m."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Kathleen A. Lord",
      "case_id": "2011-10-9995",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "6c65bc3a65ac746b14744cf76d318e3d50dca1598ddd1d6c68f05a4538ea8a0c",
      "evidence_id": "ev-6c65bc3a65ac-0000-0002",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 3,
        "start": 3
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The Tenth Circuit, after finding that Mr. Wood's petition presented two substantial constitutional claims, denied him habeas relief solely on the ground that his petition was untimely. It did this even though the State had done three things that should have precluded this result: First, the State deliberately relinquished a known statute of limitations defense. Second, in doing so, the State acted strategically, not inadvertently. And, third, in doing so, the State induced the district court to expend substantial resources in deciding claims of exhaustion and -- and deciding claims on the merits."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Scalia",
      "case_id": "2011-10-9995",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "6c65bc3a65ac746b14744cf76d318e3d50dca1598ddd1d6c68f05a4538ea8a0c",
      "evidence_id": "ev-6c65bc3a65ac-0000-0003",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 6,
        "start": 6
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Are there a lot of cases raising the -- you know, the ambiguity that exists in this case? I mean, if you tell me that's all this case is about, I think we ought to dismiss this -- dismiss it as improvidently granted."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Kathleen A. Lord",
      "case_id": "2011-10-9995",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "6c65bc3a65ac746b14744cf76d318e3d50dca1598ddd1d6c68f05a4538ea8a0c",
      "evidence_id": "ev-6c65bc3a65ac-0000-0004",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 14,
        "start": 14
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "My concern is not the relative power. It's that when a court is raising something sua sponte, it defeats the party presentation principle."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Alito",
      "case_id": "2011-10-9995",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "6c65bc3a65ac746b14744cf76d318e3d50dca1598ddd1d6c68f05a4538ea8a0c",
      "evidence_id": "ev-6c65bc3a65ac-0000-0005",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 16,
        "start": 16
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "What Rule of Civil Procedure applies here? You're talking about appellate procedure here. Is there a rule of appellate procedure that governs this?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Ms. Lord",
      "case_id": "2011-10-9995",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "6c65bc3a65ac746b14744cf76d318e3d50dca1598ddd1d6c68f05a4538ea8a0c",
      "evidence_id": "ev-6c65bc3a65ac-0001-0000",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 20,
        "start": 20
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Because if they had -- if they had challenged timeliness, they would have raised this very complicated issue, because the only way they can win on timeliness is to win on this newfangled notion of abandonment under Colorado law, which under Colorado law requires a hearing and requires factual development."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "15 States",
      "case_id": "2011-10-9995",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "6c65bc3a65ac746b14744cf76d318e3d50dca1598ddd1d6c68f05a4538ea8a0c",
      "evidence_id": "ev-6c65bc3a65ac-0001-0001",
      "kind": "amicus_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 21,
        "start": 21
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Ms. Lord, an amicus brief filed on behalf of 15 States contends that the Civil -- the Rules of Civil Procedure are not what should be consulted here, because they govern only to the extent that they're not inconsistent with habeas rules, and asserts that the -- the habeas rules should rather apply and that they -- that they cut against your case."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Ms. Lord",
      "case_id": "2011-10-9995",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "6c65bc3a65ac746b14744cf76d318e3d50dca1598ddd1d6c68f05a4538ea8a0c",
      "evidence_id": "ev-6c65bc3a65ac-0001-0002",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 22,
        "start": 22
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Well, that's the district court, and that's one of the key differences between the district court and the appellate court. The appellate court can issue a certificate of appealability. The district court has that prescreening function, which is just like the prescreening function in the PLRA, where this Court looked at a circuit's attempt to create rules that would address policy concerns and deviated and put an enhanced pleading requirement on prisoners and said, I believe unanimously, that that shouldn't be done."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Domenico",
      "case_id": "2011-10-9995",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "6c65bc3a65ac746b14744cf76d318e3d50dca1598ddd1d6c68f05a4538ea8a0c",
      "evidence_id": "ev-6c65bc3a65ac-0001-0003",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 23,
        "start": 23
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "In contrast to the new and fairly complicated set of doctrines my friend asked the Court to adopt today, this Court can resolve this case by applying two straightforward longstanding rules. First, in Granberry v. Greer, the Court recognized that courts are not bound by a State's failure to properly argue and preserve a procedural bar to a habeas claim."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Breyer",
      "case_id": "2011-10-9995",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "6c65bc3a65ac746b14744cf76d318e3d50dca1598ddd1d6c68f05a4538ea8a0c",
      "evidence_id": "ev-6c65bc3a65ac-0001-0004",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 27,
        "start": 27
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "When you say \"further,\" I want to be very precise about the distinction. You have to put it in your answer. You didn't."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Sotomayor",
      "case_id": "2011-10-9995",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "6c65bc3a65ac746b14744cf76d318e3d50dca1598ddd1d6c68f05a4538ea8a0c",
      "evidence_id": "ev-6c65bc3a65ac-0001-0005",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 29,
        "start": 29
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Can I -- can I ask you, do you mean to tell me that, using your own words in your brief, that a waiver is the intentional abandonment of a known right?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Ginsburg",
      "case_id": "2011-10-9995",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "6c65bc3a65ac746b14744cf76d318e3d50dca1598ddd1d6c68f05a4538ea8a0c",
      "evidence_id": "ev-6c65bc3a65ac-0002-0000",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 38,
        "start": 38
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE GINSBURG: There is something about the principle of party presentation. The party raises the issue. The court of appeals is the court of review, not first view."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Court",
      "case_id": "2011-10-9995",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "6c65bc3a65ac746b14744cf76d318e3d50dca1598ddd1d6c68f05a4538ea8a0c",
      "evidence_id": "ev-6c65bc3a65ac-0002-0003",
      "kind": "fact",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 38,
        "start": 38
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Official was trying to be candid with the court. It discovered this 1995 motion on its own."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Court",
      "case_id": "2011-10-9995",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "6c65bc3a65ac746b14744cf76d318e3d50dca1598ddd1d6c68f05a4538ea8a0c",
      "evidence_id": "ev-6c65bc3a65ac-0002-0004",
      "kind": "procedural_event",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 43,
        "start": 43
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MS. ARBUS SHERRY: The State did have an opportunity in the court of appeals because, after the application for a certificate of appealability was filed, the court did order the State to file a response, and the State didn't argue timeliness in that response."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Kathleen A. Lord",
      "case_id": "2011-10-9995",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "6c65bc3a65ac746b14744cf76d318e3d50dca1598ddd1d6c68f05a4538ea8a0c",
      "evidence_id": "ev-6c65bc3a65ac-0002-0002",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 50,
        "start": 50
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MS. LORD: The problems with the rulings urged by the amicus and by the State are severalfold. One, it's not contemplated at all by AEDPA that the 1-year Federal timing statute of limitations would be subject to such a wide latitude in the court of appeals to resurrect defenses."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Source extraction",
      "case_id": "2011-10-9995",
      "confidence": 0.0,
